Yes. A single function can have both a cron trigger and event triggers. Pass them together in a `triggers` array in the function config (up to 10 unique triggers per function) [1]:

```ts
inngest.createFunction(
  {
    id: "resync-user-data",
    triggers: [
      { event: "user.updated" },
      { cron: "0 5 * * *" }, // every day at 5am
    ],
  },
  async ({ event, step }) => {
    // ...
  },
);
```

If the developer was trying to set `cron` and `event` as keys on a single trigger object, that won't work. Each trigger is its own object in the array, either `{ event }` or `{ cron }`. Go uses `inngestgo.MultipleTriggers{...}` and Python takes a list of `TriggerEvent`/`TriggerCron` [1].

Inside the handler, check `event.name` to see what fired the run. Cron runs arrive as the scheduled event (`InngestScheduledEvent`), so you can branch on that [3]. If you define multiple overlapping crons, only one cron run fires per given second [4].

