Yes, this is a known class of problem, and passing `fetch` explicitly is the supported fix, not just a hack. In older SDK versions (v2/v3), `new Inngest()` fell back to `cross-fetch` when no `fetch` was supplied. Bundlers building for the Edge runtime could resolve cross-fetch's browser build, which uses the XMLHttpRequest polyfill, and that doesn't exist on Edge. Newer SDK versions use the global `fetch` when one is available, so first upgrade to the latest `inngest` package and remove the workaround to see if it's no longer needed.

If you're stuck on an older version, keep the explicit option:

```ts
export const inngest = new Inngest({
  id: "my-app",
  fetch: fetch, // use the Edge runtime's global fetch
});
```

Also, make sure your serve handler is exported with `export const runtime = "edge"` only where needed. The Next.js serve docs cover the handler setup [1]. If you have a mix of Edge and Node handlers, you can use `referenceFunction` to avoid importing Node-only function dependencies into the Edge bundle [3][4].

I can't point to a specific issue or release number for the fix from the sources here, so please check the SDK changelog for the exact version.

control.text:
Yes, this was a known issue in older versions of the `inngest` SDK (v2/early v3). When no `fetch` was passed, the SDK fell back to `cross-fetch`, and bundlers targeting Edge resolved its browser build, which uses an XMLHttpRequest-based polyfill. XHR doesn't exist in the Edge runtime, hence `ReferenceError: XMLHttpRequest is not defined`.

**Fix:** upgrade to a recent `inngest` v3 release. The SDK now uses the runtime's global `fetch` when it exists, so the `cross-fetch` fallback shouldn't be hit on Edge. I can't pin the exact version that changed this, so check the changelog for the release that dropped `cross-fetch`.

**If you can't upgrade**, your workaround is the right one:

```ts
export const inngest = new Inngest({
  id: "my-app",
  fetch: fetch, // use the Edge runtime's global fetch
});
```

Also make sure the serve handler is the one for your runtime and that you set `export const runtime = "edge";` in the route file.

